# CoT-Restoration Project Rules

- Before editing code, running tests/training, or changing Git state, state the
  tasks and a realistic time range. Start immediately after approval and move
  to the next approved task as soon as the current one finishes; never wait for
  the estimate to elapse.
- Kaggle examples must use the exact CDD-11 path
  `/kaggle/input/datasets/mintesnotfikir/cdd-11-30`. Do not use placeholders or
  a broad `/kaggle/input` data root.
- Kaggle pretrained files are under
  `/kaggle/input/datasets/hoangkhanhtung/nafnetmodel`:
  `NAFNet-GoPro-width32.pth`, `NAFNet-GoPro-width64.pth`,
  `NAFNet-SIDD-width32.pth`, and `NAFNet-SIDD-width64.pth`.
- Match checkpoint topology explicitly. GoPro and SIDD checkpoints are not
  interchangeable. Record missing, unexpected, and mismatched tensors; do not
  silently accept a partial load in a final run.
- The current Kaggle CDD-11 input is the small 30-pair-per-degradation subset.
  Use it for pipeline validation and preliminary ablation, and label results as
  subset results. Recommend the full CDD-11 before publication-level claims.
- Split by clean scene ID and reject train/validation/test scene overlap.
- Select pretrained initialization and tune hyperparameters on validation only.
  Keep `CDD-11_test` held out until the experiment configuration is locked.
- Every training run must save config, environment, Git commit, dataset
  manifest, pretrained loading report, epoch log, summary, and checkpoints.
- `notebook/nafnet.ipynb` benchmarks four pretrained models on partial GoPro and
  SIDD data; it is not a CDD-11 baseline result.
- Prefer importing the project notebook from GitHub. Its bootstrap cell clones
  `https://github.com/HoangKhanhTung0111/CoT-restoration.git`; the user should
  not need to copy individual cells.
- Kaggle T4 sessions expose two T4 GPUs while the P100 option exposes one P100.
  The user intends to select T4; training should use both visible T4 devices and
  record per-rank device memory through `DistributedDataParallel`. Do not use
  `nn.DataParallel`: it leaked one host-side model-sized buffer per optimizer
  step under Kaggle PyTorch 2.10. Do not assume evaluation batch size one
  benefits from two GPUs.
